WEBVTT

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:37.760
Taeguk refers to the source of everything in the universe and the universal law in which everything continuously goes through cycles of creation and destruction.

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:49.760
Pumse Taeguk symbolizes the root principle of universal philosophy and is constructed by applying the truth inherent in Taeguk's yin and yang principles.

00:00:49.760 --> 00:00:57.760
Taeguk Chiljang stands for Ga and symbolizes a mountain that embodies the meaning of mass and firmness.

00:00:57.760 --> 00:01:03.940
We will now take a look at the Pumse of Chiljang.

00:01:03.940 --> 00:02:11.330
Taeguk Chiljang consists of 13 key moves, namely

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:43.880
손 날 꼬들어 아래 맞기, 꼬들어 바탕손 안 맞기, 보주먹 가위맞기, 무릎치기, 해처 맞기, 두 주먹 젖어 지르기, 엇거러 아래 맞기, 등주먹 바깥치기, 표척차기, 옆지르기, 범서기,

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:50.880
and 주춤서기. These are the 13 key moves of Taeguk Chiljang.

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:56.880
We will now take a closer look at each of the key moves of Taeguk Chiljang.

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:07.700
손 날 아래 맞기 is a technique used to block, defend the lower body with your arms and with the help from an assisting hand.

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:13.700
The blocking hand is placed over the knee forward about two-fists length apart.

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:20.700
The assisting hand is raised to the same level as the elbow of the arm blocking the lower body.

00:03:20.700 --> 00:03:30.440
The wrist of the assisting arm is placed where it crosses the center line.

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:35.440
The space between the outer wrist and torso should be about one hand blade apart.

00:03:35.440 --> 00:03:39.440
The elbow should be naturally close to the torso.

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:47.710
Be careful not to let your blocking hand move outside the line connecting your leg and body.

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:52.710
The assisting armpit should not detach from your body, making the elbow rise up.

00:03:52.710 --> 00:03:59.630
Goduro Batangseon Anmaki is also a blocking technique.

00:03:59.630 --> 00:04:10.080
When initiating this move, straighten the assisting arm slightly at the solar plexus level.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:16.080
Start your blocking hand one hand's length above your shoulder and block inwards.

00:04:16.080 --> 00:04:23.990
Place the palm of your hand on the blocking arm in front of the solar plexus.

00:04:24.990 --> 00:04:31.990
The back of the assisting hand should point upward and assist by letting the elbow of the blocking arm lightly rest on it.

00:04:31.990 --> 00:04:42.220
Block by pulling the elbow of the blocking arm towards the body and pull your assisting arm.

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:56.230
Bojumon is a move to envelop the right closed fist with the left hand.

00:04:56.230 --> 00:05:02.230
The four fingers of your left hand wrap around the four fingers of your right fist.

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:09.270
The left thumb wraps around the right thumb.

00:05:09.270 --> 00:05:13.270
The space between your fists should be a sheet of paper's width.

00:05:13.270 --> 00:05:22.840
The assisting fist starts in front of the lower abdomen and is raised to the front of the philtrum passing the front of the chest.

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:29.840
The internal angle of your arm should be between 90 degrees and 120 degrees.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:38.840
Kawimaki is a maki move that performs anpalmok bakatmaki and aremaki at the same time.

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:42.840
It starts with both arms crossed in front of the chest.

00:05:42.840 --> 00:05:52.840
The wrist of the arm performing anpalmok bakatmaki should be placed at the solar plexus level and your fist must not rise above your shoulder.

00:05:52.840 --> 00:06:01.120
The fist that performed aremaki should maintain its position two raised fists above the knee.

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:07.120
Murubchigi is a technique used to kick upwards with the knee by folding the leg.

00:06:07.120 --> 00:06:12.620
Kick upwards with your knee folded as much as possible.

00:06:12.620 --> 00:06:25.270
Hechomaki is a technique used to block inside out simultaneously by crossing your wrists in front of your chest.

00:06:25.270 --> 00:06:30.580
Your fists must be in front of your shoulders when blocking.

00:06:30.580 --> 00:06:38.660
Your elbows should point downward and your two armpits should softly touch your torso.

00:06:38.660 --> 00:06:46.660
Dujumok chocho jirugi is a technique used to bend back and thrust by pointing the back of your hand downward.

00:06:46.660 --> 00:06:54.660
Your two fists start at the back with the backs of your hands facing upward and thrust downward after bending back.

00:06:54.660 --> 00:07:02.140
The key targets are jeonggwang and hyopbok pressure points.

00:07:02.140 --> 00:07:10.740
When thrusting, keep your arms in touching the torso and place your elbows close to your torso.

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:15.740
Chocho jirugi should be performed by thrusting upwards slightly from below.

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:26.210
When performing this move, the elbows should not move away from the body too much. Your fists must not open outward.

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:32.210
Be careful not to let your fists rise above the elbow level or thrust downward.

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:43.210
Otkoro aremaki is a technique used to block in front of the lower abdomen by making a diagonal loop with the two wrists.

00:07:43.210 --> 00:07:53.100
For Wen Apgubi, start with your right arm back and cross your right arm upwards.

00:07:53.100 --> 00:08:02.100
For Oren Apgubi, start with your left arm back and cross your left arm upwards.

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:07.110
Both elbows should be touching your torso.

00:08:07.110 --> 00:08:16.060
Dujumok pakachigi starts with the first of the opposite shoulder.

00:08:16.060 --> 00:08:23.060
The assisting arm should cross below the thrusting arm to initiate the preparatory move.

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:31.160
Then strike a target with the back of your fist using the torque of your torso.

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:42.260
Your fist should align with your shoulder line and your elbow should be naturally straight.

00:08:42.260 --> 00:08:49.790
Pyojokchagi is a technique used to create a target with the palm of your hand.

00:08:49.790 --> 00:08:55.830
Kick outside in using the foot blade.

00:08:55.830 --> 00:09:03.700
Be careful not to kick with the sole of your foot.

00:09:04.700 --> 00:09:12.560
Is a technique used to thrust sideways while standing sideways like Jujumsogi.

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:18.560
The flow of force progress transferred from the ground is pushed up to the fist through the elbow.

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:30.510
Bumsogi posture is achieved by shifting your weight on your hind leg

00:09:30.510 --> 00:09:38.510
and standing low after setting your front foot forward about a foot length from the tip of the big toe to your hind foot.

00:09:38.510 --> 00:09:47.700
Raise your heel so only the ball of the front foot touches the ground

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:58.300
and support your weight with your hind leg so that your weight is distributed equally on both your hind and front leg 9 to 1.

00:09:58.300 --> 00:10:04.300
Slowly tighten legs inward and stand with your knees tilted forward.

00:10:04.300 --> 00:10:10.300
Be careful not to let your upper body move too much forward or backward.

00:10:10.300 --> 00:10:14.300
The last of the 13 key movements is Jujumsogi.

00:10:14.300 --> 00:10:25.060
Jujumsogi posture is achieved by keeping the tops of your feet parallel about two feet length apart, standing low.

00:10:25.060 --> 00:10:30.440
These are the 13 key moves of Taegukchijang.

00:10:30.440 --> 00:10:34.440
We will now look at the moves in succession, connecting each move.

00:10:34.440 --> 00:10:39.880
The key moves of Taegukchijang first part consist of

00:10:39.880 --> 00:10:45.880
Orun Patangseon Anmakki, Orun Apchagi, and Wen Anmakki,

00:10:45.880 --> 00:10:52.880
Wen Patangseon Anmakki, Wen Apchagi, and Orun Anmakki,

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:59.880
Wen Seonnal Godureo Aremakki, and Orun Seonnal Godureo Aremakki.

00:10:59.880 --> 00:11:03.880
Shift your weight to your right foot from Jumbijase.

00:11:03.880 --> 00:11:09.880
Turn left using the right ball of your foot and assume the Bomsagi posture.

00:11:09.880 --> 00:11:16.880
Start the preparatory move for Anmakki while bending your knee using the momentum from Apchagi.

00:11:16.880 --> 00:11:18.880
And complete the Momtongmakki.

00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:24.880
Move the moment your heel touches the ground, followed by the ball of the forward kicking foot.

00:11:24.880 --> 00:11:30.880
Shift your weight to your front leg and at the same time spin your torso clockwise

00:11:30.880 --> 00:11:37.880
using the left ball of your foot as an axis to perform the preparatory moves for Patangseon Anmakki.

00:11:37.880 --> 00:11:45.880
Then spin and set your foot in line with the direction of progression and complete Patangseon Anmakki.

00:11:45.880 --> 00:11:48.880
Shift your weight to your hind leg.

00:11:48.880 --> 00:11:55.880
Perform Wen Duikubi by spinning your torso using the left ball of your foot and setting your right foot.

00:11:55.880 --> 00:12:03.060
Cautions for Taegukjiljang, first part, taking a closer look.

00:12:03.060 --> 00:12:14.510
When spinning 180 degrees and setting your foot from Wen Bomsagi to Orun Bomsagi,

00:12:14.510 --> 00:12:23.780
be careful not to overdo the switch to your right foot with your left foot fixed.

00:12:23.780 --> 00:12:35.060
When performing Dikki and Doradikki, it is important to understand the coordination between moving the center of the body and spinning with the torso.

00:12:35.060 --> 00:12:56.330
We will now practice the moves of Taegukjiljang, first part, in succession with watching the video.

00:12:56.330 --> 00:13:03.330
The key moves of Taegukjiljang, second part, consist of Orun Goduro Patangseon Anmakki,

00:13:03.330 --> 00:13:13.330
Orun Goduro Ddeungjungmok Apchigi, Wen Goduro Patangseon Anmakki, Wen Goduro Ddeungjungmok Apchigi, and Bojumok.

00:13:13.330 --> 00:13:28.020
After Goduro Patangseon Anmakki, perform the preparatory move as if you're pulling your blocking hand to your opposite ear.

00:13:28.020 --> 00:13:37.020
The back of the striking hand should face the ear and your elbow should not move away from the back of the hand of the assisting arm.

00:13:37.020 --> 00:13:41.020
The target for Ddeungjungmok Apchigi is the opponent's philtrum.

00:13:41.020 --> 00:13:44.020
The elbow of the striking arm should not move outward.

00:13:44.020 --> 00:13:52.020
Be careful not to lose balance due to your upper body swerving front and back, left and right.

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:59.290
Cautions for Taegukjiljang, second part, taking a closer look.

00:13:59.290 --> 00:14:07.290
When performing Goduro Patangseon Anmakki, you should block as if you're pulling your elbows towards your body.

00:14:07.290 --> 00:14:13.290
When performing Goduro Ddeungjungmok Apchigi, your elbows should not move outward.

00:14:13.290 --> 00:14:18.290
And be careful not to lose balance due to excessive swerving of your upper body.

00:14:18.290 --> 00:14:27.430
We will now practice the moves of Taegukjiljang, second part, in succession while watching the video.

00:14:27.430 --> 00:14:48.820
The key moves of Taegukjiljang, third part, consist of

00:14:48.820 --> 00:15:05.700
Gawimakki and Gawimakki, Haecheomakki, Oryunmurup Oryeochigi, and Ddujumok Cheochyeochirigi, Okkoro Aremakki.

00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:15.700
Haecheomakki, Wenmurup Oryeochigi, and Ddujumok Cheochyeochirigi, and Okkoro Aremakki.

00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:24.520
Gawimakki is a technique used to block by transferring and distributing force to both arms.

00:15:24.520 --> 00:15:31.520
The Gawimakki and Gawimakki performed in succession is the first move of Taegukjiljang, third part,

00:15:31.520 --> 00:15:36.520
and should be repeated twice from the same Sogi posture respectively.

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:44.520
When first performing Gawimakki from Wen Apgubi, perform Oren Aremakki and Wen Anpalmok Pakalmakki.

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:53.520
Then, complete the Wen Apgubi Gawimakki by following it with Wen Aremakki and Oren Anpalmok Pakalmakki in succession.

00:15:53.520 --> 00:16:02.470
Next, set your foot with Oren Apgubi, perform Wen Aremakki and Oren Anpalmok Pakalmakki,

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:09.470
and complete Oren Apgubi Gawimakki by performing Oren Aremakki and Wen Anpalmok Pakalmakki.

00:16:09.470 --> 00:16:14.780
We will now connect the Gawimakki moves.

00:16:15.780 --> 00:16:17.780
Dujumok Chochojirugi.

00:16:17.780 --> 00:16:21.780
This move is used to hold and pull an opponent's neck or head

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:26.780
and attack an opponent's face or torso with your knee at the same time.

00:16:26.780 --> 00:16:32.780
First, open your hands so they face each other, then pull them down.

00:16:32.780 --> 00:16:38.780
It is more effective if you pull your fists down to the ankle of the kicking foot.

00:16:38.780 --> 00:16:41.780
When you hold and pull an opponent down,

00:16:41.780 --> 00:16:45.780
you can exert more force if you pull your wrists downward.

00:16:45.780 --> 00:16:50.780
The pulling down of your opponent and Murubchigi should be performed simultaneously.

00:16:50.780 --> 00:16:55.780
When initiating the preparatory moves for Okoro Aremakki from the right side,

00:16:55.780 --> 00:17:01.780
make a diagonal loop by moving the right wrist up and left wrist down.

00:17:01.780 --> 00:17:04.780
When initiating from the left side,

00:17:04.780 --> 00:17:10.780
do the opposite and make a diagonal loop by moving the left wrist up and right wrist down.

00:17:10.780 --> 00:17:16.740
Both wrists should be placed in front of the lower abdomen,

00:17:16.740 --> 00:17:19.740
and both elbows should be tightened inward.

00:17:19.740 --> 00:17:24.330
Cautions for Taegukchirijang.

00:17:24.330 --> 00:17:27.330
Third part, taking a closer look.

00:17:27.330 --> 00:17:36.540
Be careful not to move the upper body excessively when performing Gawimakki in succession.

00:17:36.540 --> 00:17:42.540
When performing Murubchigi, do not pull while the back of your hands are facing upward.

00:17:42.540 --> 00:17:53.380
During Okoro Aremakki, do not straighten your elbow or let your armpit detach from your body.

00:17:53.380 --> 00:18:02.380
We will now practice the moves of Taegukchirijang, third part, in succession while watching the video.

00:18:02.380 --> 00:18:28.260
The key moves of Taegukchirijang, fourth part, consist of

00:18:38.260 --> 00:18:50.260
Taegukchirijang, fourth part.

00:18:50.260 --> 00:18:55.260
In order to connect the last move of Taegukchirijang, third part, that you just learned,

00:18:55.260 --> 00:19:01.260
Wen Apgubi Okoro Aremakki, to the first move of Taegukchirijang, fourth part,

00:19:01.260 --> 00:19:03.260
Wen Ddeungjumeok Bakgachigi,

00:19:03.260 --> 00:19:08.260
the preparatory move must be performed first by crossing the left arm over the right arm

00:19:08.260 --> 00:19:11.260
so it is placed on the right shoulder.

00:19:11.260 --> 00:19:17.260
Then perform Ddeungjumeok Bakgachigi at shoulder height by utilizing the spin of the torso.

00:19:17.260 --> 00:19:22.260
The elbow should straighten naturally and be careful not to let the back of the fist

00:19:22.260 --> 00:19:25.260
move away from the end of your shoulder line.

00:19:25.260 --> 00:19:30.260
Byojeokchagi is a technique used to kick a target with the back of the foot blade.

00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:36.260
During Baegumchi Byojeokchigi, the target must not wrap around the elbow.

00:19:36.260 --> 00:19:42.260
It is important to perform Byojeokchagi and Byojeokchigi in succession.

00:19:42.260 --> 00:19:47.260
This is how you connect Byojeokchigi and Ddeungjumeok Bakgachigi.

00:19:47.260 --> 00:19:53.700
Raise your posture by straightening the knees from the Juchumseogi position.

00:19:53.700 --> 00:19:58.700
Change direction by moving and setting the right foot at the same time.

00:19:58.700 --> 00:20:03.700
Then perform Ddeungjumeok Bakgachigi using the spin of the torso.

00:20:03.700 --> 00:20:07.700
The right foot transitions to Apsugi,

00:20:07.700 --> 00:20:11.700
and the left foot is pulled slightly as your center moves forward.

00:20:11.700 --> 00:20:21.790
Cautions for Taegukjiljang, fourth part, taking a closer look.

00:20:21.790 --> 00:20:27.790
Be careful not to bend the elbow when performing Ddeungjumeok Bakgachigi.

00:20:27.790 --> 00:20:33.790
The target is the palm of the hand when performing Byojeokchigi.

00:20:33.790 --> 00:20:39.740
Be careful not to let the target move.

00:20:39.740 --> 00:20:45.740
We will now practice the moves of Taegukjiljang, fourth part, in succession while watching the video.

00:20:45.740 --> 00:21:02.920
This concludes the training for Taegukjiljang.

00:21:02.920 --> 00:21:06.920
We will take another look at all the moves and master Poomsae.

00:21:06.920 --> 00:22:13.600
We have looked at the key moves of Taegukjiljang

00:22:13.600 --> 00:22:18.600
and provided thorough explanations for each move and things to watch out for.

00:22:18.600 --> 00:22:22.600
You will be able to learn much better postures and techniques

00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:27.600
if you continue training with the spirit and firmness that symbolize Taegukjiljang.

00:22:27.600 --> 00:22:33.260
We will see you again in the Taegukpaljang video.

00:22:33.260 --> 00:22:35.260
Thanks for watching.

